"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[548] \"That's good,\" Quine approved. \"So, when was the last time you saw Monty Field?\" [549] Morgan cleared his throat uneasily. \"Er... I haven't seen him in a while,\" he whispered in response. \"You see, we used to be partners - successful in legal matters. Then some things happened, and we went our separate ways. Since then, I... I haven't seen him again.\" [550] \"How long ago was that?\" [551] \"About two years and a bit.\" [552] \"Very good.\" Quinn leaned forward. \"I also want to know why you split up.\" [553] Morgan looked down at the carpet, fiddling with his cigar. \"I... uh, I guess you know what kind of person Field is. We had a disagreement about ethics, and after a small argument, we decided to go our separate ways.\" [554] \"Did you part on good terms?\" [555] \"Uh... in that situation, yes.\" [556] Quinn tapped the table. Morgan shifted uncomfortably. He clearly hadn't fully recovered from the shock. [557] \"What time did you arrive at the theater tonight, Morgan?\" the officer asked. [558] Morgan seemed surprised by the question. \"Ah\u2014around 8:15,\" he replied. [559] \"Let me see your ticket stub,\" Quinn said. [560] Morgan fumbled through his pockets before producing it and handing it to Quinn. Quinn took the ticket stub, then pulled out three more from his pockets, placing them under the table and carefully comparing them. He then returned the four ticket stubs to his pocket without expression. [561] \"So, you were sitting in the middle M2 seat, right?\" he said. \"Nice spot, Morgan. What made you want to see 'Gunplay'?\" [562] \"Ah, it's a novelty show, isn't it, officer?\" Morgan looked a bit embarrassed. \"I didn't even know I'd want to come to the theater \u2013 you know, I don't usually go to the theater \u2013 but the manager of the Roman Theater sent me a free ticket for tonight's performance, so I came.\" [563] \"Is that so?\" Quine asked bluntly. \"When did you receive the ticket?\" [564] \"Ah, I got the letter on Saturday morning, officer, delivered to my office.\" [565] \"Oh, you got a letter too, did you? You don't happen to have it on you, do you?\" [566] \"I\u2014very\u2014sure\u2014I brought it,\" he mumbled, starting to rummage through his pockets, \"Here it is! Here you go.\" [567] He handed the officer a small rectangular piece of rough-edged paper made of wrinkled securities printing paper. Officer Quinn carefully took it to the light. Through the printed lines, a clear watermark pattern could be seen. He pursed his lips and carefully placed the paper on the desk's notepad. Under Morgan's gaze, he opened the drawer above Posen's desk and searched until he found a sheet of paper. The paper was large and square, with a luxurious theater seal printed on it. Quinn placed the two papers side by side, pondered for a moment, then sighed, picked up the one Morgan handed him, and read it slowly. [568] The Roman Theater Department cordially invites Benjamin Morgan, Esq. to attend the performance of 'Gunfight' on September 24th (Monday) evening. We sincerely hope that Mr. Morgan, a leading figure in the legal profession in New York, will share his valuable opinions on the social and legal aspects of the play. Of course, we do not impose any obligation. The Roman Theater Department guarantees that Mr. Morgan can accept this invitation without any obligation. [569] (Signature) Roman Theater\n[570] Inviter: S.\n[571] The \"S\" written in ink was sloppy and almost unrecognizable. [572] Quin looked up, smiled. \"Roman Theatre is quite something, Mr. Morgan. I was wondering -\" he continued to smile, gesturing towards Johnson, who had been sitting quietly in the corner, listening to their conversation. [573] \"Call the manager, Johnson,\" Quin said, \"if that publicity man - that Bellson, or Pearson, or whoever - is here, have him come too.\" [574] After Johnson left, Quin turned back to Morgan. [575] \"Could you show me your gloves, Mr. Morgan?\" he whispered. [576] Morgan looked at him perplexed and then threw the gloves on the table in front of him. Quinn picked them up curiously. They were a pair of white silk gloves \u2014 typically worn with evening attire. Quinn pretended to be busy examining the gloves. He turned them over, closely inspecting a smudge on one fingertip, and even tried to put them on his own hand, joking with Morgan. After his examination, he returned the gloves to Morgan with a serious expression. [577] \"Ah \u2014 oh, by the way, Mr. Morgan \u2014 your hat is quite a sight. May I see it?\" [578] Morgan placed the hat on the table, still silent. Quinn picked it up nonchalantly, whistling a tune called \"Broadway,\" a few notes lower than standard pitch. He flipped the hat in his hand. It was a high-quality hat, with a lustrous silk lining and a gold stamp of the manufacturer, \"James Chalmers & Co.\" The initials \"B.M.\" were embroidered on the band. [579] Quinn grinned and put the hat on his own head. It fit perfectly. But he quickly took it off and handed it back to Morgan. [580] \"Thank you very much for allowing me to examine your gloves and hat, Mr. Morgan,\" he said as he pulled out a notepad from his pocket and jotted down a note hastily. [581] The door opened, and Johnson, Pensel, and Harry Nelson entered. Pensel hesitated before approaching, while Nelson sat in an armchair. [582] \"What can I do for you, officer?\" Pensel asked nervously, trying to ignore the dejected, silver-haired figure sitting in the chair. [583] \"Mr. Pensel,\" Quinn said calmly, \"In your Roman Theatre, how many kinds of writing paper do you have?\" [584] The manager's eyes widened. \"Just one, officer. There's one right in front of you on the table.\" [585] \"Ah \u2013\" Quinn handed Pensel the paper he had taken from Morgan. \"I want you to take a good look at this paper, Mr. Pensel. Do you know if the Roman Theatre has such paper?\" [586] The manager gave the paper a strange look. \"No, I don't think we have such paper. In fact, I'm quite sure. What does it say?\" He turned around and called out, \"Nelson! What is this?\u2014Is it your latest promotional gimmick?\" He waved the paper in front of Nelson. [587] Nelson took the paper from the manager and quickly read it. \"Ah, it seems I'll have to change careers,\" he whispered. \"This is bound to break the advertising record.\" He read it again, his face showing envy. Then, under the gaze of four reproachful eyes, he handed the paper back to Pencier. \"I'm sorry, such a brilliant idea didn't even occur to me,\" he said slowly, retreating to the corner and crossing his arms. [588] The manager turned to Quine with a puzzled expression. \"This is strange, officer. As far as I know, the Roman Theater never uses such stationery, and I'm quite certain I never authorized anyone to do such promotional work. If Nelson says he didn't do it\u2014\"\n[589] Quine carefully put the paper in his pocket. \"Alright, thank you, gentlemen,\" he said, nodding at Pencier and Nelson as they left. [590] He looked at Morgan, who was flushed with anger and embarrassment. Quine raised his hand and slammed it lightly on the table again. [591] \"What do you think of this, Mr. Morgan?\" He asked straightforwardly. [592] Morgan jumped up. \"Damn it, this is a frame-up!\" He waved his fist in front of Quinn, \"Please excuse my rudeness, I know no more about this than you do. And don't think you can scare me by searching my hat and gloves - oh, you haven't searched my underwear yet, Inspector!\" [593] \"But my dear Morgan,\" Inspector Quinn said gently, \"why do you have to make yourself so nervous? Others would think I accused you of murdering Monte Field. Sit down, calm down, old boy. I'll ask you a simple question.\" [594] Morgan slumped in the chair, wiped his forehead with a trembling hand, and whispered, \"Sorry, Inspector, I couldn't control my temper. But these unfortunate things -\" He calmed down and murmured. [595] Inspector Quinn sat there, staring at him with a puzzled look. Morgan was busy wiping his sweat with a handkerchief and puffing on a cigar. Johnson coughed disapprovingly and looked up at the ceiling. Suddenly, there was a commotion in the room, but it soon quieted down. [596] Quin's voice broke the silence. \"Well, that's all, Mr. Morgan. You can go now.\" [597] Morgan slowly rose, opened his mouth to say something, but closed it again, put on his hat, and left the room. Johnson saw the officer's gesture and lazily stood up, casually opening the door for him. Both men left. [598] The room was left with Quin alone, who immediately got to work. He pulled out four ticket stubs, the letter Morgan had given him, and the lady's rhinestone handbag he had found in the dead man's pocket. He opened the handbag again, took out its contents, and laid them on the table in front of him. There were several business cards with \"Frances Eves-Popp\" printed elegantly; two delicate handkerchiefs; a small makeup case containing foundation, blush, and lipstick; a small money pouch containing twenty-dollar bills and some coins; and a door key. Quin toyed with these items for a moment, put them back in the handbag, and put the handbag, ticket stubs, and letter back in his pocket. He stood up, slowly scanning the room, then walked over to the coat rack, picked up a top hat hanging there, and checked its lining. It had the initials \"L.P.\" printed on it, and the hat size was 6 3/4, which piqued his interest. [599] He put the hat back in its place, opened the door. [600] The four people sitting in the reception room immediately stood up, their faces expressing relief. Quin stood at the door, smiling, with his hands in his coat pockets. [601] \"It's finally your turn,\" he said. \"Come on, everyone, come into the office.\" [602] He politely stepped aside to let them go first \u2013 three ladies and a young man. They all entered the office with some excitement, the young man hurrying to pull chairs for the ladies to sit down. Four pairs of eyes eagerly looked at the elderly man standing at the door. Quein smiled, like a fatherly figure. He quickly glanced at the reception room, then closed the door, and sternly walked to the table and sat down, reaching for his snuffbox. [603] \"Alright!\" he said mildly. \"I'm very sorry to have kept you waiting so long \u2013 official business, you know. Now, let's get started. Well, right, right, I must \u2013 well, now, first of all, ladies and this gentleman, how shall we begin? I believe, young lady, although I haven't had the pleasure of hearing you introduce yourself, your name should be Frances Eves-Pope, is that right?\" [604] The girl raised her eyebrows. \"Yes, sir,\" her voice was lively and melodious, \"although I'm not sure how you know my name.\" [605] She smiled. Her smile was attractive and seductive, and her gaze was captivating. She was in her prime, graceful and charming, with brown, large eyes and a complexion like jade. She exuded vitality that revitalized Quein's spirits. [606] He smiled at her. \"Well, Miss Eves-Pope, I think for most people, your identity would be a mystery. But I'm a policeman, so I'm stronger than most people, so it's not difficult for me to know your name. And you're definitely not an unknown girl, in fact, I saw your photo in the social section of the newspaper today.\" [607] The girl smiled, a little nervous. \"Oh, so that's how it is!\" She said, \"I'm starting to get scared. Sir, what do you want with me?\" [608] \"Official business - always official business,\" Detective Quin said pitifully, \"I'm interested in anyone, I always relate it to my profession... before asking you questions, I want to ask, who are your friends?\" [609] When Detective Quin looked at the three of them, all three coughed awkwardly. Miss Francis smiled charmingly. \"I apologize, sir, please allow me to introduce my friends. These two ladies are Hilda Orangi and Eve Ellis, my good friends; this is Mr. Stephen Barry, my fianc\u00e9.\" [610] Detective Quin looked at them with a touch of surprise. \"If I'm not mistaken, you're all members of the cast of 'Gunfight', right?\" [611] All three nodded in unison. [612] Queene turned to Francis. \"I don't mean to pry, Miss Eves-Pope, but I want you to explain... why your friends are here with you?\" [613] The three actors stood up very awkwardly. Francis looked from her companions to Queene, pleading with her eyes. [614] \"I\u2014I'm sorry, officer,\" she said quickly. \"I... I've never been questioned by the police before. I'm nervous, and I want my fianc\u00e9 and these two ladies\u2014my closest friends\u2014to be here with me when you ask me questions. I didn't realize it went against your wishes...\u201d\n[615] \"I understand,\" said Queene, smiling. \"I completely understand. But, you see\u2014\" he made a gesture for them to go out first. [616] Stephen Barry leaned over the girl's chair, a challenging look in his eyes as he stared at Quinn. \"Darling, I won't leave if you need me.\" [617] \"But, Stephen, dear\u2014\" Frances helplessly sobbed. Quinn's expression remained firm, showing no signs of relenting. \"You...you should go out first. But please wait outside for me. It won't be long, right, sir?\" she asked, her eyes showing displeasure. [618] Quinn shook his head. \"Not too long.\" His attitude had changed completely, seeming to become somewhat irritable. The other three seemed to sense his change, subtly aligning themselves against him. [619] Hilda Orlandi was a tall, plump woman in her forties, still attractive despite her age, with traces of her youthful beauty on her face. Under the cold light in the room, her face lost its color. She leaned over Frances, staring at Detective Quinn with a firm tone in her voice. \"We'll wait for you outside, dear, if you feel uncomfortable or anything, just call out, and we'll come right in to help you.\" She huffed out of the room, followed by Eve Ellis, who patted Frances' hand comfortingly. \"Don't worry, Frances, we're all here for you.\" She tugged on Barry's arm and followed Hilda Orlandi out. Barry glanced back at Quinn, a mix of anger and concern in his eyes. He glared at Quinn again before slamming the door shut. [620] Quinn immediately stood up, his movements clean and his attitude cold. He stared into Frances' eyes, his hand resting on the surface of the table. \"Now, Frances Eves-Pope, Miss,\" he said succinctly, \"I'd like to talk to you about this...\u201d He reached into his pocket, pulling out a rhinestone purse like a stage magician. \"I'd like to return your purse to you.\" [621] Francis sat up a little, her gaze shifting from Detective Quinn to the sparkling handbag. Her face turned pale. \"Oh, that's... that's my evening bag!\" [622] \"Indeed, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Quinn said, \"it was found at the theater\u2014tonight.\" [623] \"Of course!\" She smiled nervously and sat back down. \"I'm such a fool! I didn't even know my bag was missing until now...\"\n[624] \"But, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Detective Quinn continued solemnly, \"where we found your bag is more important than finding the bag itself.\" [625] Francis opened her mouth wide, staring at him with fear in her eyes. \"Yes, I heard about it.\" She whispered. [626] \"Well, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Quinn coldly continued, \"your bag was found in the pocket of the deceased!\" [627] Fear filled the girl's eyes. She let out a sob and swayed on her chair, her face pale and tense. [628] Quinn leaped forward, a look of pity and concern on his face. He touched Francis's weak body just as the door burst open and Stephen Barry rushed into the room, his coat flapping behind him. Hilda Orlancy, Eve Ellis, and Johnson followed close behind. [629] \"What did you do to her, you damned detective!\" Barry shouted, shoving Quinn aside. He gently held Francis in his arms, brushing aside the strand of black hair that was in front of her face and whispering comforting words in her ear. Francis sighed and looked up at the agitated face next to her, confused. \"Stephen, I...I feel dizzy,\" she murmured, and then sank back into Stephen's embrace. [630] \"Bring some water!\" the young man exclaimed, stroking her hand. Johnson immediately handed over a flat glass cup. Barry forced a few gulps down her throat, and Francis coughed and regained her senses. The two actresses pushed Barry aside and sternly ushered the men out of the room. Quinn followed the protesting young man and the detective out the door with a submissive expression. [631] \"You're a good cop, aren't you?\" Barry sniped at Quinny. \"What did you do to her \u2013 give her a whack on the head with your usual police tactics?\" [632] \"Oh, oh, young man,\" Quinny said mildly, \"don't talk so harshly. She was just frightened.\" [633] They all stood there in silence, the tension palpable. The door opened, and the two actresses emerged, supporting Francis. Barry rushed to her side, stroking her hand and whispering, \"Do you feel better now, dear?\" [634] \"Stephen \u2013 take me \u2013 home,\" she gasped, leaning on Barry. [635] Quinny stepped aside, letting them pass. He watched them slowly make their way to the front door, accompanied by a small crowd of spectators. [636] Chapter Six: The District Attorney Becomes a Biographer\n[637] Detective Richard Quinn was a unique man. Although small in stature, he was remarkably sturdy; his head was full of white hair, and his face was etched with lines that spoke of his rich life experiences.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."